### Active-high tri-state buffer

The note's circuit has data input `a`, enable input `en` (no inversion bubble), and output `z`.

```text
           |\
a ---------| >------ z
           |/
            |
en ---------+
```

| en | a | z |
| :---: | :---: | :---: |
| 0 | 0 | Z |
| 0 | 1 | Z |
| 1 | 0 | 0 |
| 1 | 1 | 1 |

The note's concurrent assignment passes `a` only when `en = '1'` and drives `'Z'` otherwise. The testbench uses the p. 12 input transitions so the output waveform can be compared with the expected values.

```vhdl
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity tristate is
    port (a, en : in std_logic; z : out std_logic);
end entity tristate;

architecture exm1 of tristate is
begin
    z <= a when en = '1' else 'Z';
end architecture exm1;

library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity test_tristate is
end entity test_tristate;

architecture io of test_tristate is
    signal p, q, r : std_logic;
begin
    g1: entity WORK.tristate(exm1)
        port map (a=>p, en=>q, z=>r);

    p <= '1', '0' after 100 ns, '1' after 250 ns, '0' after 500 ns;
    q <= '1', '0' after 130 ns, '1' after 280 ns, '0' after 460 ns;

end architecture io;
```

### Decoder testbench

The last sentence of the printed question says “decoder.” The notes treat the tri-state gate and the 2-to-4 decoder as separate examples. Use the decoder on pp. 12–13 for that sentence. Its one-hot outputs, in order `Z3 Z2 Z1 Z0`, are `0001`, `0010`, `0100`, and `1000` for inputs `00`, `01`, `10`, and `11`. The testbench below extends the note's timed stimulus to cover all four binary inputs.

```vhdl
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity decoder2to4 is
    port (
        a : in  std_logic_vector(1 downto 0);
        z : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
    );
end entity decoder2to4;

architecture exm1 of decoder2to4 is
begin
    z <= "0001" when a = "00" else
         "0010" when a = "01" else
         "0100" when a = "10" else
         "1000" when a = "11" else
         "XXXX";
end architecture exm1;

library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity test_decoder2to4 is
end entity test_decoder2to4;

architecture io of test_decoder2to4 is
    signal ain  : std_logic_vector(1 downto 0);
    signal zout : std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
begin
    g1: entity WORK.decoder2to4(exm1)
        port map (a=>ain, z=>zout);

    ain <= "00", "01" after 150 ns, "10" after 300 ns, "11" after 450 ns;

end architecture io;
```

### (b) Blink an LED at 200 Hz

Assume an Arduino Mega 2560 at 16 MHz, Timer3 available for this sketch, and an active-high low-current LED from **PA4 (D26)** to ground through a suitable series resistor. Take 200 Hz to mean 200 complete ON–OFF cycles per second with a 50% duty cycle. The full period is 5 ms, so toggle every 2.5 ms.

Choose CTC mode and a prescaler of 64. The timer clock is $16,000,000/64=250,000 mathrm{Hz}$. One half-period takes $250,000 	imes 2.5 mathrm{ms}=625$ ticks. The CTC interval is $mathrm{OCR3A}+1$ ticks, so set `OCR3A = 624`.

```text
Time (ms)    0        2.5       5.0       7.5      10.0
LED          LOW ─────┐ HIGH ────┐ LOW ─────┐ HIGH ────┐
                      toggle     toggle     toggle     toggle
```

This adapts the notes’ Timer3 CTC polling sketch to 200 Hz. Timer3 runs continuously so each compare match supplies the next toggle interval.

```cpp
#define outPin 4  // PA4

void T3delay();

void setup() {
  PORTA &= ~(1<<outPin);
  DDRA |= (1<<outPin);
  TCCR3B = 0x00;
  TCCR3A = 0x00;
  TIMSK3 = 0x00;
}

void loop()
{
  T3delay();
  PORTA ^= (1<<outPin);
}

void T3delay()
{
  OCR3AH = 0x02;
  OCR3AL = 0x70;
  TCNT3H = 0x00;
  TCNT3L = 0x00;
  TIFR3 = (1<<OCF3A);  // Clear a pending flag by writing 1
  TCCR3B = 0x0B;        // CTC, clock / 64

  while ((TIFR3 & (1<<OCF3A)) == 0) {}
  TCCR3B = 0x00;
  TIFR3 = (1<<OCF3A);
}
```

### (a) Foreground and timer-interrupt LEDs

**Assumptions.** Arduino Mega 2560 at 16 MHz, with active-high LEDs on PA7 (D29) and PA3 (D25). Each LED has a series current-limiting resistor and returns to ground. As worded, 400 Hz is the **toggle rate** of LED 1 and 100 Hz is the **full blink-cycle rate** of LED 2.

```text
Main loop   ───────────→ PA7 (D29) → resistor → LED 1 → GND
Timer3 Normal → OVF ISR → PA3 (D25) → resistor → LED 2 → GND
```

- **LED 1.** The main loop toggles PA7 once every $1/400=2.5\,\mathrm{ms}$. One on/off cycle therefore takes $5\,\mathrm{ms}$ (200 cycles/s).
- **LED 2.** Toggle every $5\,\mathrm{ms}$ for a 100 Hz cycle. Timer3 uses Normal mode and prescaler 64, so each tick is $4\,\mu\mathrm{s}$. Load $65536-5000/4=64286$, or `0xFB1E`, into `TCNT3H:TCNT3L`. Reload that value in the overflow ISR, as in tutorial 16. The software reload and foreground instructions add small timing overhead.

```cpp
#include <avr/interrupt.h>

#define mainPin 7       // PA7
#define interruptPin 3  // PA3

void setup() {
  cli();
  PORTA &= ~(1<<mainPin);
  DDRA |= (1<<mainPin);
  PORTA &= ~(1<<interruptPin);
  DDRA |= (1<<interruptPin);

  TCCR3B = 0x00;
  TCCR3A = 0x00;         // Normal mode
  TCNT3H = 0xFB;
  TCNT3L = 0x1E;
  TIFR3 = (1<<TOV3);
  TIMSK3 = (1<<TOIE3);
  TCCR3B = 0x03;         // Clock / 64
  sei();
}

void loop()
{
  delayMicroseconds(2500);
  PINA = (1<<mainPin);  // Atomic bit toggle; ISR also uses PORTA
}

ISR(TIMER3_OVF_vect)
{
  TCNT3H = 0xFB;
  TCNT3L = 0x1E;
  PINA = (1<<interruptPin);  // Write one to toggle only PA3
}
```

Writing each toggle mask directly to `PINA` lets the main loop and ISR change separate output bits without a shared read-modify-write of `PORTA`.

### (b) Count external pulses with Timer/Counter5

Use the note's **T5 input on PL2 (Mega D47)**, a common ground, and a logic-level pulse source. Keep Timer/Counter5 in 16-bit Normal mode and set `CS52:0 = 110` to count one falling edge per pulse. `TCNT5` holds the full 16-bit count. Output its lower byte on `PORTA` (D22–D29), as in the notes.

```text
Pulse source → PL2 / T5 (D47) → edge detector → TCNT5
                                                │ low byte
                                                ▼
                                           PORTA (D22–D29)
```

```cpp
void setup() {
    TCCR5A = 0;
    TCCR5B = 0;
    TIMSK5 = 0;              // No Timer5 interrupt is needed.
    DDRL &= ~(1<<PL2);
    PORTL &= ~(1<<PL2);     // External source actively drives T5.
    PORTA = 0;
    DDRA = 0xFF;

    TCNT5H = 0x00;
    TCNT5L = 0x00;
    TIFR5 = (1<<TOV5);       // Clear any old overflow indication.
    TCCR5B = 0x06;  // External T5, falling edge.
}

void loop() {
    PORTA = TCNT5L;  // Display the low byte

    if (TIFR5 & (1<<TOV5)) {
        TIFR5 = (1<<TOV5);   // Write one to clear this flag.
    }
}
```

The displayed byte wraps after 256 edges. The full hardware count wraps after 65,536 edges. For a larger cumulative count, accumulate Timer5 overflows in software.

### (b) Continuously sample ADC0 and output to an 8-bit port

Assume a 16 MHz Mega 2560. Connect a signal within the reference range to **PF0 (A0)** and share ground. Use AVCC as the reference with the usual capacitor on AREF. Configure `PORTA` (D22–D29) for output. The notes poll `ADIF` and read a right-adjusted result. For this 8-bit output, set `ADLAR = 1` so a single read of `ADCH` provides bits 9–2 of the result.

```cpp
void setup() {
    PORTA = 0;
    DDRA = 0xFF;
    DDRF &= ~(1<<PF0);
    PORTF &= ~(1<<PF0);     // Disable the internal pull-up on ADC0.

    ADCSRB = 0x00;            // MUX5 = 0.
    ADMUX = 0x60;  // AVCC, left-adjusted, ADC0.
    ADCSRA = 0x87 | (1<<ADIF);  // Enable ADC, clear stale flag.
}

void loop() {
    ADCSRA |= (1<<ADSC);
    while (!(ADCSRA & (1<<ADIF))) {
        // Wait for a new conversion.
    }

    PORTA = ADCH;
    ADCSRA = 0x87 | (1<<ADIF);  // Write one to clear ADIF.
}
```

### (a) Two-switch logic selector

Assume the two selector switches drive `PC1` and `PC0`, with `PC1` as the most significant selector bit. Connect each switch from its input to +5 V and use a pull-down resistor to ground, so an open switch gives 0 and a closed switch gives 1. Take the two logic operands from driven inputs `PC2` and `PC3`. Use `PC4` as the result output. All connections share the microcontroller ground. On the Mega board, `PC0` to `PC4` are D37 through D33, in descending order.

```text
+5 V ─ switch ─┬─ PC1 or PC0  (one circuit per selector)
               └─ 10 kΩ ─ GND
A ──────────────── PC2
B ──────────────── PC3
Y ←──────────────── PC4
```

| PC1 PC0 | Operation | Output Y |
| :---: | :--- | :--- |
| 00 | AND | `a & b` |
| 01 | OR | `a \| b` |
| 10 | XOR | `a ^ b` |
| 11 | NAND | `!(a & b)` |

**Arduino Mega 2560 sketch.** Read `PINC` once per loop, mask the selector bits, and extract the two operands as 0 or 1. Update only the output bit in `PORTC`.

```cpp
#define selectorMask ((1<<PC1) | (1<<PC0))
#define inputMask (selectorMask | (1<<PC2) | (1<<PC3))
#define outputMask (1<<PC4)

void setup() {
    DDRC &= ~inputMask;
    PORTC &= ~(inputMask | outputMask);  // No internal pull-ups; Y starts low.
    DDRC |= outputMask;
}

void loop() {
    unsigned char pins = PINC;
    unsigned char mode = pins & selectorMask;
    unsigned char a = (pins >> PC2) & 1U;
    unsigned char b = (pins >> PC3) & 1U;
    unsigned char y;

    switch (mode) {
        case 0: y = a & b;  break;
        case 1: y = a | b;  break;
        case 2: y = a ^ b;  break;
        default: y = !(a & b);  // mode 3
    }

    if (y) PORTC |= outputMask;
    else   PORTC &= ~outputMask;
}
```

### (b) Even-parity indicator

Assume all eight bits are driven on `PA7:PA0`. The LED turns on if the data byte contains an even number of 1s, including zero 1s. There is no separate parity bit in this question. Use D22–D29 for `PA0:PA7` and D30 for `PC7`. Starting from 0, XOR each bit into the running parity. The final value is 0 for even and 1 for odd.

```text
Data byte ──→ PA7:PA0
PC7 ── current-limiting resistor ── LED (anode to resistor) ── GND
```

**Arduino Mega 2560 sketch.** Run this independently of part (a).

```cpp
#define ledMask (1<<PC7)

void setup() {
    DDRA = 0x00;
    PORTA = 0x00;           // The external source drives all eight inputs.
    PORTC &= ~ledMask;     // Active-high LED starts off.
    DDRC |= ledMask;
}

void loop() {
    unsigned char data = PINA;
    unsigned char parity = 0;
    for (unsigned char i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
        parity ^= data & 1U;
        data >>= 1;
    }

    if (parity == 0) PORTC |= ledMask;
    else             PORTC &= ~ledMask;
}
```
